1
00:00:00,550 --> 00:00:05,460
The 2026 Nobel Prize in Literature goes
to the Canadian author Anne Carson.

2
00:00:06,508 --> 00:00:10,348
The Swedish Academy awards it
for her bold and inventive oeuvre that,

3
00:00:10,468 --> 00:00:13,138
in playful dialogue
with the classical tradition,

4
00:00:13,278 --> 00:00:16,138
has created new forms
for contemporary literature.

5
00:00:17,211 --> 00:00:19,811
The classical tradition
reaches her in pieces:

6
00:00:19,911 --> 00:00:23,791
of more than 120 plays
by Sophocles, seven survive;

7
00:00:23,936 --> 00:00:28,381
of an estimated 9,000 to 10,000
lines by Sappho, about 650.

8
00:00:29,294 --> 00:00:31,864
Most of what remains
of Sappho is fragments.

9
00:00:32,006 --> 00:00:34,904
When editors print a torn
papyrus, square brackets mark

10
00:00:34,904 --> 00:00:35,824
the missing letters,

11
00:00:35,824 --> 00:00:37,754
and inside them goes their guess.

12
00:00:37,944 --> 00:00:41,078
Carson's own Sappho keeps
the brackets and the blank space.

13
00:00:43,322 --> 00:00:44,377
For 2,000 years,

14
00:00:44,377 --> 00:00:46,042
Sappho has been translated:

15
00:00:46,232 --> 00:00:47,332
Catullus into Latin,

16
00:00:47,332 --> 00:00:49,662
then English versions from 1711 on,

17
00:00:49,662 --> 00:00:51,262
each with different choices.

18
00:00:53,291 --> 00:00:54,977
Anne Carson trained in Classics,

19
00:00:54,977 --> 00:00:57,151
with a doctorate in 1981.

20
00:00:57,395 --> 00:00:58,558
She translates Sappho,

21
00:00:58,558 --> 00:01:01,853
and tragedies by Sophocles,
Euripides and Aeschylus.

22
00:01:02,747 --> 00:01:05,418
Her essay Eros the Bittersweet, from 1986,

23
00:01:05,418 --> 00:01:07,348
draws on a compound word of Sappho's:

24
00:01:07,348 --> 00:01:10,193
Eros as pain and sweetness combined.

25
00:01:14,171 --> 00:01:15,143
Across the centuries,

26
00:01:15,143 --> 00:01:17,091
she sets voices side by side:

27
00:01:17,221 --> 00:01:19,001
Simonides with Paul Celan,

28
00:01:19,001 --> 00:01:20,821
Thucydides with Virginia Woolf.

29
00:01:21,840 --> 00:01:25,154
The Glass Essay is a long
poem that is also a story,

30
00:01:25,154 --> 00:01:29,280
built around three women at a kitchen
table; genre boundaries blur.

31
00:01:30,709 --> 00:01:32,242
Autobiography of Red,

32
00:01:32,242 --> 00:01:33,251
a novel in verse,

33
00:01:33,251 --> 00:01:35,752
starts from ancient Greek
fragments about Geryon,

34
00:01:35,752 --> 00:01:37,122
a winged red monster,

35
00:01:37,182 --> 00:01:40,362
and makes him the tender hero
of a modern coming-of-age story.

36
00:01:43,293 --> 00:01:44,533
In The Beauty of the Husband,

37
00:01:44,533 --> 00:01:47,663
a fictional essay unfolds in 29 tangos,

38
00:01:47,733 --> 00:01:50,101
each opening with lines by John Keats.

39
00:01:51,026 --> 00:01:52,986
Nox is a book in a box:

40
00:01:53,235 --> 00:01:56,645
an accordion fold-out
of pictures, letters and texts,

41
00:01:56,754 --> 00:01:58,544
in memory of her brother.

42
00:01:59,614 --> 00:02:01,541
And Float is 22 chapbooks,

43
00:02:01,541 --> 00:02:03,824
small booklets
that can be read in any order:

44
00:02:03,854 --> 00:02:06,674
more than a billion
trillion possible sequences.

45
00:02:07,867 --> 00:02:08,614
Beyond the page,

46
00:02:08,614 --> 00:02:09,843
she writes opera libretti,

47
00:02:09,843 --> 00:02:12,788
and works with artists,
choreographers and directors.

48
00:02:13,892 --> 00:02:16,362
She is the 19th woman
to receive the prize,

49
00:02:16,362 --> 00:02:19,062
and the second Canadian,
after Alice Munro.

50
00:02:20,056 --> 00:02:21,456
In the Academy's words:

51
00:02:21,456 --> 00:02:23,826
new forms for contemporary literature.

52
00:02:25,266 --> 00:02:27,396
This film was made with showtime.
